#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078164 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078577 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21b4ce3-d80e-5894-92ce-668e64d91649" :knowledge-point/title "Clearing a Rational Expression With a Variable on One Side"}] :question/id #uuid "7adb1612-0903-5a50-b5cd-75ebf1d0dd4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12854" :question/problem "Solve the equation $13 = \\dfrac{6y+8}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 13 &= \\dfrac{6y+8}{2} \\\\ 2 \\cdot 13 &= 2 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{6y+8}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 13 &= \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{6y+8}{\\cancel{2}}\\right) \\\\ 26 &= 6y+8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 26 &= 6y+8 \\\\ 26-8 &= 6y+8-8 \\\\ 18 &= 6y \\ \\dfrac{18}{6} &= \\dfrac{6y}{6} \\\\ 3 &= y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $y=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078165 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078578 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d31a29c9-e053-557b-8f5a-3dd49b340144" :knowledge-point/title "Clearing a Rational Expression With a Variable on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "c176f07d-e4ee-5f8a-8b7c-7b1c62898bd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12855" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{a+2}{3} =a$." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{a+2}{3} &=a \\\\ 3 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{a+2}{3} \\right) &= 3 \\cdot a \\ \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{a+2}{\\cancel{3}} \\right) &= 3a \\\\ a+2 &= 3a \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} a+2 &= 3a \\\\ a + 2 - a &= 3a - a \\\\ 2 &= 2a \\ \\dfrac{2}{2} &= \\dfrac{2a}{2} \\\\ 1 &= a \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078579 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9d60309-32e0-535d-b5fa-ef55787336c8" :knowledge-point/title "Clearing a Rational Expression With Binomials on Both Sides"}] :question/id #uuid "31f9d893-26fd-5b2e-9730-57c5e6cc0615" :question/math-academy-id "e-12857" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{2n+6}{3} = 4n+1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3$. We must make sure that *all* of the terms on the right-hand side are multiplied by $3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2n + 6}{3} &= 4n + 1 \\\\\n3 \\cdot (\\frac{2n + 6}{3}) &= 3 \\cdot (4n + 1) \\\\\n3 \\cdot (\\frac{2n + 6}{3}) &= 3 \\cdot 4n + 3 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n2n + 6 &= 12n + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2n + 6 &= 12n + 3 \\\\\n2n + 6 - 2n &= 12n + 3 - 2n \\\\\n6 &= 10n + 3 \\\\\n6 - 3 &= 10n + 3 - 3 \\\\\n3 &= 10n \\\\\n\\frac{3}{10} &= \\frac{10n}{10} \\\\\n\\frac{3}{10} &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\nn = \\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078167 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078580 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f22f7b2-16a9-5742-9bef-c26d774c85de" :knowledge-point/title "Clearing a Rational Expression That Includes a Factored Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "de18238b-ec8a-525d-b886-b869aa736d56" :question/math-academy-id "e-12858" :question/problem "Solve $\\dfrac{5p+10}{6} =2(p-5)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{5p+10}{6} &=2(p-5) \\\\ 6 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{5p+10}{6}\\right) &=6\\cdot 2 (p-5) \\ \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{5p+10}{\\cancel{6}}\\right) &=12(p-5) \\\\ 5p+10 &=12p-60 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 5p+10 &=12p-60 \\\\ 5p+10-5p &=12p-60-5p \\\\ 10 &=7p-60 \\\\ 10+60 &=7p-60+60 \\\\ 70 &=7p \\ \\dfrac{70}{7} &=\\dfrac{7p}{7} \\\\ 10 &= p \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $p=10$."}]}
